WEBVTT

00:00:00.656 --> 00:00:04.359
In this tutorial, we will take one Julia
performance question

00:00:04.359 --> 00:00:07.638
through a complete investigation in VS
Code. Our small

00:00:07.638 --> 00:00:11.159
package has a sum of squares operation and
a waiting task.

00:00:11.290 --> 00:00:15.378
We will run a check, inspect saved
evidence, compare configurations,

00:00:15.378 --> 00:00:19.225
explore a notebook, and discuss a possible
change with an agent.

00:00:19.356 --> 00:00:22.251
Then we will review an isolated
implementation,

00:00:22.251 --> 00:00:25.331
apply it deliberately, validate it, and
keep a way

00:00:25.331 --> 00:00:28.534
back. PerfChecker Studio opens in a full
editor tab.

00:00:28.665 --> 00:00:31.303
You can place it beside the code, split
the editor,

00:00:31.303 --> 00:00:33.786
and keep a result visible while you
investigate.

00:00:33.917 --> 00:00:38.207
The activity bar is an entry point; the
working surfaces include editor

00:00:38.207 --> 00:00:42.497
tabs, the Testing view, output channels
and a dedicated Julia terminal.

00:00:42.497 --> 00:00:46.546
The important habit is to retain the
evidence behind each decision.

00:00:46.677 --> 00:00:50.565
A successful command tells us that an
action completed. To decide

00:00:50.565 --> 00:00:54.932
whether a change is useful, we also need
its correctness result, measured

00:00:54.932 --> 00:00:59.419
quantity, units and configuration. That is
the workflow we will build here.

00:00:59.785 --> 00:01:03.870
There are two parts to this workspace. The
frontend selects an action,

00:01:03.870 --> 00:01:07.956
presents its configuration, displays
progress and reads saved results.

00:01:08.087 --> 00:01:12.247
The Julia backend constructs the requested
experiment and executes it

00:01:12.247 --> 00:01:16.347
using the selected environments. The
controller contains PerfChecker

00:01:16.347 --> 00:01:20.205
and the collectors or interface packages
needed for that action.

00:01:20.337 --> 00:01:23.991
A measurement or analyzer worker needs the
code under test and

00:01:23.991 --> 00:01:27.705
its own dependencies. The editor is not
evidence that those two

00:01:27.705 --> 00:01:31.714
environments are interchangeable, so we
will select them explicitly.

00:01:31.845 --> 00:01:35.703
Fresh workers keep an earlier interactive
session from silently

00:01:35.703 --> 00:01:39.500
becoming the state of our experiment.
Saved reports retain the

00:01:39.500 --> 00:01:43.665
observations and configuration needed to
interpret the result later.

00:01:43.796 --> 00:01:48.488
The frontend can display what was
recorded; it cannot invent a missing

00:01:48.488 --> 00:01:53.515
profile, analyzer result or baseline. MCP
adds another explicit connection.

00:01:53.515 --> 00:01:58.140
PerfChecker calls a configured agent tool
with the requested context.

00:01:58.272 --> 00:02:01.875
Advice, implementation and measurement
remain separate actions,

00:02:01.875 --> 00:02:05.593
so an answer can be reviewed before any
proposed code is applied.

00:02:06.521 --> 00:02:09.772
The tutorial targets the corrected one
point zero point

00:02:09.772 --> 00:02:13.022
one releases of the core and extension.
Check that both

00:02:13.022 --> 00:02:16.214
are available before following the
installation steps.

00:02:16.345 --> 00:02:19.779
The core comes from Julia General, and the
editor extension

00:02:19.779 --> 00:02:23.388
comes from the Visual Studio Marketplace.
Installing the Julia

00:02:23.388 --> 00:02:26.881
package and installing the extension are
two separate steps.

00:02:27.012 --> 00:02:31.308
Open the package folder that you want to
measure. In a workspace containing

00:02:31.308 --> 00:02:35.432
several folders, select the intended
package each time the command asks.

00:02:35.563 --> 00:02:39.068
Trust a workspace when you are prepared to
execute its Julia

00:02:39.068 --> 00:02:42.924
code, including suite factories and
notebook cells. This recording

00:02:42.924 --> 00:02:46.546
uses a qualification workspace prepared
for the demonstration.

00:02:46.677 --> 00:02:50.957
Its release report identifies the exact
source tree and editor platform.

00:02:51.088 --> 00:02:55.282
For your own first installation, follow
the registered-package recipe in

00:02:55.282 --> 00:02:59.708
the documentation, and check the active
project and resolved package version

00:02:59.708 --> 00:03:03.785
rather than assuming that an older
environment upgraded automatically.

00:03:04.150 --> 00:03:08.456
Start by preparing a controller that you
can inspect. In an unconfigured

00:03:08.456 --> 00:03:12.522
Studio, choose Set up workspace and create
suite. You can create the

00:03:12.522 --> 00:03:17.067
controller environment, select an existing
project, or open the setup guide.

00:03:17.198 --> 00:03:20.959
Package downloads require an explicit
installation confirmation.

00:03:20.959 --> 00:03:24.602
The controller provides PerfChecker and
its measurement tools.

00:03:24.734 --> 00:03:29.148
The package being measured still needs its
own dependencies, and existing

00:03:29.148 --> 00:03:33.320
test items need their test dependencies in
the selected runner. After

00:03:33.320 --> 00:03:37.371
setup, inspect Runner Project and the
resolved PerfChecker version.

00:03:37.502 --> 00:03:41.631
For an MCP connection, add HTTP
deliberately to the controller.

00:03:41.631 --> 00:03:45.367
Pluto uses a separate project with the
qualified notebook

00:03:45.367 --> 00:03:48.971
dependencies. Opening Studio gives you the
next action.

00:03:49.103 --> 00:03:52.775
It does not request measurements or
install an agent. A clear

00:03:52.775 --> 00:03:56.988
environment selection makes the later
result much easier to reproduce.

00:03:57.354 --> 00:04:00.312
Here are the environment settings for this
folder.

00:04:00.312 --> 00:04:03.566
Runner Project selects the controller.
Scenario Project

00:04:03.566 --> 00:04:06.880
selects the environment used by shared
scenario workers.

00:04:07.011 --> 00:04:10.168
Pluto Project selects the separate
notebook interface

00:04:10.168 --> 00:04:13.326
environment. The suite path and reports
path tell the

00:04:13.326 --> 00:04:17.020
extension where to read the experiment and
retain its results.

00:04:17.151 --> 00:04:21.179
These paths can be relative to the
selected package folder. Folder

00:04:21.179 --> 00:04:25.452
settings are especially useful when
several packages share one VS Code

00:04:25.452 --> 00:04:29.541
window. Check the selection before running
an expensive experiment.

00:04:29.672 --> 00:04:33.400
The dedicated PerfChecker terminal starts
the selected Julia

00:04:33.400 --> 00:04:37.624
executable with the controller project.
Use it to inspect the active

00:04:37.624 --> 00:04:41.476
project and package status, or
deliberately call a public API.

00:04:41.607 --> 00:04:45.548
Its interactive state is separate from
fresh measurement workers.

00:04:45.548 --> 00:04:49.307
The Julia extension's own REPL and
debugger use their selected

00:04:49.307 --> 00:04:53.732
environment, so inspect that selection as
well when moving between tools.

00:04:54.660 --> 00:04:57.771
We will begin with the Vector reduction
test item from

00:04:57.771 --> 00:05:00.710
the guide. It checks that summing the
integers from

00:05:00.710 --> 00:05:03.879
one to ten thousand gives fifty million,
five thousand.

00:05:04.011 --> 00:05:07.692
The runner needs TestItemRunner version
one point three point

00:05:07.692 --> 00:05:11.132
two or newer, along with TestItems and the
package's test

00:05:11.132 --> 00:05:15.236
dependencies. Discover the items in the
Testing view and select one.

00:05:15.367 --> 00:05:19.431
Tags can narrow the performance selection,
and explicit repetitions

00:05:19.431 --> 00:05:23.616
control how many fresh processes are
requested. There is no automatic

00:05:23.616 --> 00:05:27.377
warmup of a native test item. Read the
item before running it.

00:05:27.508 --> 00:05:30.259
Its setup, operations and correctness
assertion

00:05:30.259 --> 00:05:32.599
define the scope that is being observed.

00:05:32.730 --> 00:05:36.310
If an item builds data, performs file
access or includes

00:05:36.310 --> 00:05:40.018
several operations, do not describe its
result as the cost

00:05:40.018 --> 00:05:43.598
of one isolated function without checking
that boundary.

00:05:43.729 --> 00:05:47.477
Run the selected item and wait for its
final status. A passing

00:05:47.477 --> 00:05:51.527
oracle gives us a useful correctness
observation for this workload.

00:05:51.658 --> 00:05:54.885
We will inspect the evidence before
interpreting its performance,

00:05:54.885 --> 00:05:57.913
then move to a separate sum of squares
workload in the suite.

00:05:58.279 --> 00:06:02.357
A green test item is a correctness result
for its declared workload.

00:06:02.357 --> 00:06:06.255
To read the performance evidence, open the
PerfChecker test items

00:06:06.255 --> 00:06:10.034
output channel and follow the Evidence
path to its JSON report.

00:06:10.165 --> 00:06:14.448
Inspect the item's validation and each
sample's seconds, bytes, correctness

00:06:14.448 --> 00:06:18.217
and completion status. The Testing view's
elapsed time can include

00:06:18.217 --> 00:06:22.329
worker startup, while the saved sample has
its own measurement boundary.

00:06:22.460 --> 00:06:26.518
Keep that report if you intend to compare
a later change. Native

00:06:26.518 --> 00:06:31.020
test-item JSON and a completed suite
report have separate result paths.

00:06:31.151 --> 00:06:33.731
For distributions, profile stacks or a
version

00:06:33.731 --> 00:06:36.759
series, prepare and run the corresponding
suite check.

00:06:36.890 --> 00:06:41.931
This gives you a reproducible first step:
one selected workload, an explicit oracle,

00:06:41.931 --> 00:06:46.491
a completed sample and a saved report
whose units and scope you can inspect.

00:06:46.857 --> 00:06:50.198
Before collecting more samples, decide
what question this

00:06:50.198 --> 00:06:53.657
result can answer. A functional item that
checks an API may

00:06:53.657 --> 00:06:57.116
be useful as a shared correctness and
performance workload.

00:06:57.247 --> 00:07:01.548
A test intended only for functional
behavior may be inappropriate for

00:07:01.548 --> 00:07:05.912
a performance budget. The tag guide
explains the supported separation.

00:07:05.912 --> 00:07:10.711
Repetitions add both scope and cost
because native items use fresh processes.

00:07:10.843 --> 00:07:14.476
A larger repetition count is not a
substitute for choosing

00:07:14.476 --> 00:07:18.924
representative input or understanding
startup and collector boundaries.

00:07:18.924 --> 00:07:23.247
A failure, timeout or unavailable
dependency is part of the evidence.

00:07:23.378 --> 00:07:27.213
Inspect the diagnostic and selected
project before treating an

00:07:27.213 --> 00:07:31.666
incomplete run as a performance
observation. Keep the package's existing

00:07:31.666 --> 00:07:36.181
correctness tests alongside any smaller
benchmark workload you introduce.

00:07:36.313 --> 00:07:41.495
The next step is a suite. It lets us state
check types, targets and comparison policy

00:07:41.495 --> 00:07:45.824
explicitly, and save the observations
needed for richer visual results.

00:07:46.752 --> 00:07:50.285
Use Create feature suite for a starter, or
point the extension

00:07:50.285 --> 00:07:53.362
at an existing suite and its factory. The
Julia source

00:07:53.362 --> 00:07:56.895
declares the workload and the combinations
that are available.

00:07:57.027 --> 00:08:00.721
The visual designer presents that plan for
selection. The tree

00:08:00.721 --> 00:08:04.177
follows package, feature, check type and
target. A feature

00:08:04.177 --> 00:08:07.573
might represent one operation or one part
of the package.

00:08:07.705 --> 00:08:12.110
Each check asks for a particular kind of
evidence, and each target identifies

00:08:12.110 --> 00:08:16.573
a measured configuration of the code.
Choose the sum of squares feature first.

00:08:16.705 --> 00:08:19.958
Inspect its input preparation and
correctness oracle in

00:08:19.958 --> 00:08:23.388
source, then check the visible execution
plan. The waiting

00:08:23.388 --> 00:08:27.056
task supplies another workload for
examining waiting behavior.

00:08:27.187 --> 00:08:32.153
The designer can only offer combinations
made available by the suite and prepared

00:08:32.153 --> 00:08:36.813
environment. The saved selection can be
reused across compatible interfaces.

00:08:36.944 --> 00:08:41.157
The Save configuration button writes the
selected visual configuration.

00:08:41.157 --> 00:08:44.716
Inspect its destination and retain the
suite source with the

00:08:44.716 --> 00:08:48.276
configuration when the experiment is meant
to be maintained.

00:08:48.642 --> 00:08:52.628
Choose a check that answers the question.
A benchmark or Chairmarks

00:08:52.628 --> 00:08:56.256
check provides timing observations for its
declared workload.

00:08:56.256 --> 00:08:59.647
Allocation measurements help investigate
memory activity.

00:08:59.778 --> 00:09:03.800
A recorded profile provides sampled call
paths. Wall profiling has

00:09:03.800 --> 00:09:07.700
its own Julia version requirement and
includes waiting behavior.

00:09:07.700 --> 00:09:11.295
Different observations have different
units and boundaries.

00:09:11.427 --> 00:09:15.194
Allocation totals, sampled allocation
sites and profile weights

00:09:15.194 --> 00:09:19.140
are related evidence, but they are not
interchangeable quantities.

00:09:19.272 --> 00:09:22.897
A network collector also has an
attribution scope, such as a

00:09:22.897 --> 00:09:26.461
shared interface or an isolated
environment. Read the check

00:09:26.461 --> 00:09:30.026
description and any unavailable reason
before selecting it.

00:09:30.157 --> 00:09:33.773
Installing a backend, analyzer or system
dependency is an

00:09:33.773 --> 00:09:37.262
explicit environment task. A collector
missing from the

00:09:37.262 --> 00:09:40.751
prepared environment has not silently
passed the check.

00:09:40.882 --> 00:09:44.587
For a before-and-after comparison, keep
the input, collector and

00:09:44.587 --> 00:09:48.408
important runtime settings comparable.
Start with one question and

00:09:48.408 --> 00:09:52.172
the smallest plan that produces the
evidence needed to answer it.

00:09:52.537 --> 00:09:55.965
Filtering changes what you can see.
Selection changes what

00:09:55.965 --> 00:09:59.511
will run. Those are separate controls in
the suite designer.

00:09:59.511 --> 00:10:02.998
Select a workload, then narrow the search
or target filter.

00:10:03.129 --> 00:10:07.870
A selected run can move outside the
visible filter and still remain selected.

00:10:07.870 --> 00:10:12.241
Read the counter for selected runs,
visible runs and selections outside

00:10:12.241 --> 00:10:16.489
the filter. Select visible and Clear
visible act on the current view.

00:10:16.620 --> 00:10:20.566
Clear selection affects the complete
selection. Show more reveals

00:10:20.566 --> 00:10:24.876
additional workload groups without
silently restricting bulk selection.

00:10:24.876 --> 00:10:28.943
Before choosing Run selected, inspect the
exact identifier preview.

00:10:29.074 --> 00:10:32.466
That is the experiment you are
authorizing, including hidden

00:10:32.466 --> 00:10:35.914
selections. Save the configuration when
you want to reuse it.

00:10:36.045 --> 00:10:38.802
This small check prevents a convenient
filter from

00:10:38.802 --> 00:10:41.504
becoming an accidental change to your
experiment.

00:10:42.431 --> 00:10:46.502
Targets let us compare a known revision
with a candidate. Choose a

00:10:46.502 --> 00:10:50.943
discovered branch, tag or commit, or enter
a supported reference. A full

00:10:50.943 --> 00:10:55.569
commit identifier is useful when you want
an unambiguous recorded baseline.

00:10:55.700 --> 00:10:59.924
Inspect the resolved target information
before running. The Example

00:10:59.924 --> 00:11:03.959
hello workload also gives us a real saved
package-version series

00:11:03.959 --> 00:11:08.183
to inspect. A Git target selects a version
of the measured package.

00:11:08.314 --> 00:11:12.157
The Julia executable separately selects
the runtime. Keep that

00:11:12.157 --> 00:11:16.434
distinction in mind when comparing package
versions across a changing

00:11:16.434 --> 00:11:20.649
environment. Use an exact baseline for a
straightforward comparison.

00:11:20.780 --> 00:11:24.634
Grouped references can use an aggregation
such as median, mean,

00:11:24.634 --> 00:11:28.916
minimum or maximum. Choose the policy that
answers your question, then

00:11:28.916 --> 00:11:33.075
inspect the comparison matrix for missing
or overlapping references.

00:11:33.206 --> 00:11:36.514
Retain the baseline and its configuration.
Changing the

00:11:36.514 --> 00:11:40.242
branch label later does not recreate a
compatible observation.

00:11:40.373 --> 00:11:44.487
The saved report should identify what was
actually measured, so that another

00:11:44.487 --> 00:11:48.437
person can assess the comparison rather
than relying on its display name.

00:11:48.803 --> 00:11:51.840
Run the reviewed selection and watch its
progress. The

00:11:51.840 --> 00:11:54.877
controller prepares the requested work,
and the result

00:11:54.877 --> 00:11:58.364
becomes useful when the run and its
observations are complete.

00:11:58.495 --> 00:12:02.564
Worker output provides diagnostics when
setup or execution fails.

00:12:02.564 --> 00:12:06.634
Cancel is an explicit request to interrupt
active work and finish

00:12:06.634 --> 00:12:10.578
cleanup. Wait for the final status before
starting another run.

00:12:10.709 --> 00:12:14.967
A notification accepting cancellation is
not the same as a completed

00:12:14.967 --> 00:12:19.037
cleanup. Closing an execution view or
stopping a notebook session

00:12:19.037 --> 00:12:22.795
can also request cancellation of work
owned by that surface.

00:12:22.926 --> 00:12:27.728
The qualified paths must wait for their
workers and clean up owned temporary

00:12:27.728 --> 00:12:32.278
allocation traces. Existing unrelated
traces are retained. If a deadline

00:12:32.278 --> 00:12:36.511
forces the controller to stop, inspect the
warning and diagnostics.

00:12:36.643 --> 00:12:40.248
Cleanup may be unconfirmed, and an
inventory may be needed for

00:12:40.248 --> 00:12:43.911
recovery. Save the error and a
reproducible workload instead of

00:12:43.911 --> 00:12:47.748
treating a closed window as proof that
every process has finished.

00:12:48.676 --> 00:12:53.204
Select a completed suite report and open
its visual output. This surface reads

00:12:53.204 --> 00:12:58.021
saved suite measurements, so it can remain
beside the source while you investigate.

00:12:58.152 --> 00:13:01.063
Choose the package, feature and check
filters that

00:13:01.063 --> 00:13:04.264
match your question. A distribution shows
the spread of

00:13:04.264 --> 00:13:07.407
recorded samples, including outliers and
sample count.

00:13:07.538 --> 00:13:11.070
Hover a point or focus its accessible
control to inspect

00:13:11.070 --> 00:13:14.855
the value and unit. The raw JSON and
series JSON provide the

00:13:14.855 --> 00:13:18.829
underlying recorded data when you need to
check its provenance.

00:13:18.961 --> 00:13:22.475
A wide distribution can motivate another
experiment, but it

00:13:22.475 --> 00:13:25.930
does not identify the cause by itself.
Check the workload,

00:13:25.930 --> 00:13:29.861
collector boundary and environment before
deciding what to change.

00:13:29.992 --> 00:13:33.433
Keep the report containing the baseline.
After modifying the

00:13:33.433 --> 00:13:36.701
code, collect the same kind of evidence
for the candidate

00:13:36.701 --> 00:13:39.740
and assess compatibility before
interpreting a ratio.

00:13:39.871 --> 00:13:43.195
A saved result is a concrete observation
tied to its

00:13:43.195 --> 00:13:47.222
configuration, not a guarantee about every
workload or machine.

00:13:47.587 --> 00:13:51.042
An overlay makes different metrics
readable together by setting

00:13:51.042 --> 00:13:54.168
each metric's minimum to one. That
normalization helps us

00:13:54.168 --> 00:13:57.513
see a pattern, but the quantities still
have their own units.

00:13:57.644 --> 00:14:01.522
A ratio of two for time and a ratio of two
for bytes describe

00:14:01.522 --> 00:14:05.210
different observations. Open the
corresponding raw version

00:14:05.210 --> 00:14:09.215
series to inspect its values, units and
recorded configuration.

00:14:09.346 --> 00:14:13.432
Hover the point, read its collector, and
check the comparison baseline.

00:14:13.432 --> 00:14:17.173
The chart can show only the evidence
present in the saved report.

00:14:17.305 --> 00:14:21.144
A missing configuration remains
unmeasured, and a demonstration

00:14:21.144 --> 00:14:25.287
dataset remains a demonstration. Use the
overlay to find a question.

00:14:25.418 --> 00:14:29.557
Use compatible raw observations,
correctness results and a repeatable

00:14:29.557 --> 00:14:33.336
workload to support the answer. An
attractive curve is a useful

00:14:33.336 --> 00:14:37.295
starting point for investigation, not the
final performance claim.

00:14:38.223 --> 00:14:41.407
A flame graph needs recorded profile
stacks. Select the

00:14:41.407 --> 00:14:44.532
profiling result from the workload that
produced them,

00:14:44.532 --> 00:14:47.658
then inspect the visible call paths and
their weights.

00:14:47.790 --> 00:14:52.143
The horizontal weight represents the
profile's sampled activity according to

00:14:52.143 --> 00:14:56.553
that collector. It is not an independent
timing measurement of each function.

00:14:56.684 --> 00:15:00.604
An operation that appears in a wide stack
is a place to investigate,

00:15:00.604 --> 00:15:04.120
especially when it aligns with the
question and workload, but

00:15:04.120 --> 00:15:07.521
the graph does not establish a speedup for
a proposed edit.

00:15:07.652 --> 00:15:12.300
Follow the available source information to
inspect the operation and its inputs.

00:15:12.431 --> 00:15:16.477
Use the Julia debugger for state or
correctness questions when helpful, then

00:15:16.477 --> 00:15:20.416
collect performance evidence separately
from stepping through the program.

00:15:20.548 --> 00:15:25.269
For allocation views, check whether the
report contains totals or sampled sites.

00:15:25.269 --> 00:15:30.167
For wall profiles, remember that waiting
can be part of the recorded task behavior.

00:15:30.298 --> 00:15:32.463
The check guide explains the boundary and

00:15:32.463 --> 00:15:34.944
provider requirements for each kind of
profile.

00:15:35.309 --> 00:15:38.909
Turn the profile into a smaller question.
Which input reaches

00:15:38.909 --> 00:15:42.272
this call path? What correctness result
must be retained?

00:15:42.272 --> 00:15:45.518
Which metric would change if the
hypothesis were right?

00:15:45.649 --> 00:15:49.711
Write or select a declared workload that
answers that question. Preserve

00:15:49.711 --> 00:15:53.377
the meaningful setup and choose an oracle
that checks the result.

00:15:53.508 --> 00:15:57.618
Include representative inputs, and
consider empty or boundary cases

00:15:57.618 --> 00:16:01.850
before requesting a rewrite. A profile can
guide that choice, while a

00:16:01.850 --> 00:16:06.388
benchmark, allocation check or analyzer
supplies another kind of evidence.

00:16:06.520 --> 00:16:09.972
Keep their results separate enough to
explain the conclusion

00:16:09.972 --> 00:16:13.711
clearly. If the report lacks the required
stacks or observations,

00:16:13.711 --> 00:16:17.336
collect them explicitly and retain the
resulting configuration.

00:16:17.467 --> 00:16:20.109
The next notebook surface gives us a
convenient

00:16:20.109 --> 00:16:22.470
place to make this investigation editable.

00:16:23.398 --> 00:16:28.116
Choose New Pluto notebook to create an
official PerfChecker dashboard as a

00:16:28.116 --> 00:16:32.962
saved Julia file. Select a feature suite
for checks and saved suite reports,

00:16:32.962 --> 00:16:37.234
or an investigation for discovery,
scenarios, diagnosis and advice.

00:16:37.366 --> 00:16:42.207
The integration prepares a separate Pluto
environment only after explicit

00:16:42.207 --> 00:16:47.049
confirmation. The qualified Pluto release
has a versioned HTTP dependency

00:16:47.049 --> 00:16:51.559
range, while the MCP controller uses its
own compatible environment.

00:16:51.691 --> 00:16:55.505
Keep the notebook interface separate from
the selected measurement

00:16:55.505 --> 00:16:59.204
workers. The real Pluto interface opens in
a VS Code editor tab.

00:16:59.336 --> 00:17:04.094
Choose the intended checks and targets,
inspect the plan, then use Launch selected

00:17:04.094 --> 00:17:08.620
checks. Refresh the job status and save
completed reports when they are ready.

00:17:08.751 --> 00:17:11.689
An investigation notebook provides its own
launch,

00:17:11.689 --> 00:17:15.039
cancellation and evidence controls.
Opening the dashboard

00:17:15.039 --> 00:17:18.448
or changing a selector does not request a
new measurement.

00:17:18.579 --> 00:17:22.167
Pluto still executes reactive Julia cells,
so inspect an

00:17:22.167 --> 00:17:25.627
unfamiliar notebook before opening it as
trusted code.

00:17:25.992 --> 00:17:29.769
A Pluto investigation is saved Julia
source that you can edit.

00:17:29.769 --> 00:17:33.364
Expand a cell, change its code, and submit
it to update the

00:17:33.364 --> 00:17:37.263
reactive output. Pluto saves the cell back
to the notebook file.

00:17:37.394 --> 00:17:40.720
That source save is separate from Save
completed reports,

00:17:40.720 --> 00:17:44.046
which retains finished suite evidence.
Keep both when you

00:17:44.046 --> 00:17:47.256
want an editable investigation and a
measured baseline.

00:17:47.387 --> 00:17:51.092
Use Open Pluto notebook to resume the
saved file. Reopening

00:17:51.092 --> 00:17:54.546
an active notebook reveals its existing
tab and server.

00:17:54.546 --> 00:17:58.063
After a stopped session, the saved source
is used again.

00:17:58.195 --> 00:18:01.829
Selecting a different check does not
silently request another

00:18:01.829 --> 00:18:05.404
measurement. For checks, use the explicit
Launch, Cancel and

00:18:05.404 --> 00:18:09.158
Refresh controls. For source edits, use
Pluto's reactive cells.

00:18:09.290 --> 00:18:12.407
This separation lets you explore the
explanation while

00:18:12.407 --> 00:18:15.582
retaining control over the experiment you
actually run.

00:18:15.947 --> 00:18:20.527
Open source reveals the saved notebook in
the Julia editor. Stop the session

00:18:20.527 --> 00:18:25.408
before editing that source externally,
then restart it to load the saved changes.

00:18:25.539 --> 00:18:29.200
Unsaved editor buffers and Pluto's saved
cells have separate

00:18:29.200 --> 00:18:32.983
lifecycles. Stop session ends the
notebook's managed Julia and

00:18:32.983 --> 00:18:36.583
Pluto session while retaining its saved
source and reports.

00:18:36.715 --> 00:18:41.074
Restart session starts the saved notebook
again. Closing the notebook tab

00:18:41.074 --> 00:18:45.434
or removing its workspace folder also
requests cleanup of its owned work.

00:18:45.565 --> 00:18:48.958
Pluto's homepage can shut down one running
notebook after

00:18:48.958 --> 00:18:53.006
confirmation while its server remains
available. Use Restart session

00:18:53.006 --> 00:18:56.757
in the PerfChecker header to reopen the
saved source afterward.

00:18:56.888 --> 00:19:00.440
For active PerfChecker work, shutdown and
restart must cancel the

00:19:00.440 --> 00:19:04.101
owned jobs and wait for cleanup before
closing the notebook worker.

00:19:04.232 --> 00:19:06.873
Inspect the final status and the
PerfChecker

00:19:06.873 --> 00:19:09.633
Pluto output if an operation reports an
error.

00:19:09.765 --> 00:19:13.845
Keep the saved report for completed work,
and use the session controls

00:19:13.845 --> 00:19:17.984
deliberately rather than sharing the
notebook's private connection URL.

00:19:18.912 --> 00:19:22.712
Investigations connect declared scenarios
with measurements

00:19:22.712 --> 00:19:25.868
and available diagnostics. Discovery
inspects the

00:19:25.868 --> 00:19:29.411
workspace or test declarations and
proposes candidates.

00:19:29.542 --> 00:19:32.989
Review the proposed case and provide its
explicit factory and

00:19:32.989 --> 00:19:36.663
correctness oracle before treating it as a
measurable experiment.

00:19:36.794 --> 00:19:41.365
Select the target environment containing
the measured package, dependencies

00:19:41.365 --> 00:19:45.875
and requested analyzers. A tool that is
unavailable is an explicit result.

00:19:46.006 --> 00:19:49.422
It does not establish that the program is
free of inference

00:19:49.422 --> 00:19:52.663
or allocation problems. Measure the chosen
case, inspect

00:19:52.663 --> 00:19:56.021
its correctness and observations, and
retain the evidence.

00:19:56.152 --> 00:19:59.563
Deterministic advice remains available
without an agent. A

00:19:59.563 --> 00:20:03.151
bounded investigation can request declared
experiments within

00:20:03.151 --> 00:20:06.974
its count and time limits; review those
limits before running it.

00:20:07.105 --> 00:20:11.545
This gives an agent conversation useful
context: what was actually tested,

00:20:11.545 --> 00:20:15.684
which tools ran, what they observed, and
which questions remain open.

00:20:15.815 --> 00:20:18.386
The next step is to discuss that evidence
without

00:20:18.386 --> 00:20:21.376
turning the first answer into an automatic
source change.

00:20:22.304 --> 00:20:26.455
Open Advisor chat and choose a connection.
The local Codex connector

00:20:26.455 --> 00:20:30.727
checks a supported native executable and
existing authentication, then

00:20:30.727 --> 00:20:34.817
provides separate advice and
implementation tools for this session.

00:20:34.948 --> 00:20:38.572
The controller needs HTTP. For an external
MCP agent,

00:20:38.572 --> 00:20:42.607
configure its endpoint and revision,
discover the available

00:20:42.607 --> 00:20:46.573
tools, and select the tool that accepts an
advice request.

00:20:46.705 --> 00:20:50.395
Use the actual case-sensitive name and
input schema. Additional

00:20:50.395 --> 00:20:53.501
required arguments belong in the
configuration, while

00:20:53.501 --> 00:20:56.957
credentials come from the configured
environment mechanism.

00:20:57.088 --> 00:20:59.851
A successful probe checks the connection
and tool

00:20:59.851 --> 00:21:02.951
inventory. The quality of an answer still
needs review.

00:21:03.082 --> 00:21:06.866
The server's filesystem permissions and
model provider remain part of

00:21:06.866 --> 00:21:10.923
your setup, and the configured provider
may process the requested context.

00:21:11.054 --> 00:21:15.540
Disconnect the temporary local connection
to return to saved provider settings.

00:21:15.906 --> 00:21:19.755
Select the saved evidence that matches
your question and ask the

00:21:19.755 --> 00:21:23.544
agent what could explain the observation.
The local advice tool

00:21:23.544 --> 00:21:27.513
uses read-only mode. Its answer gives you
a hypothesis to inspect.

00:21:27.644 --> 00:21:30.722
Continue the conversation with the
constraints that

00:21:30.722 --> 00:21:33.739
matter: empty inputs, representative
types, signed

00:21:33.739 --> 00:21:37.118
values, numerical tolerance or a specific
Julia version.

00:21:37.249 --> 00:21:41.261
Ask what experiment would test the
explanation and which oracle should

00:21:41.261 --> 00:21:45.330
preserve correctness. The follow-up can
retain the earlier messages, so

00:21:45.330 --> 00:21:49.399
the investigation becomes more precise
instead of restarting each turn.

00:21:49.530 --> 00:21:53.513
Keep observed facts separate from the
proposed explanation. After

00:21:53.513 --> 00:21:57.374
review, you can make the change yourself
or request an isolated

00:21:57.374 --> 00:22:00.928
implementation. Receiving advice does not
approve an edit.

00:22:01.059 --> 00:22:03.835
The useful next step is an explicit
validation

00:22:03.835 --> 00:22:06.611
plan tied to your workload and saved
baseline.

00:22:06.977 --> 00:22:09.958
Text advice is a proposed explanation.
Inspect

00:22:09.958 --> 00:22:13.134
its references, assumptions and validation
steps.

00:22:13.265 --> 00:22:17.012
An unavailable measurement or analyzer
should remain unavailable

00:22:17.012 --> 00:22:20.642
in the conversation, rather than acquiring
an invented result.

00:22:20.773 --> 00:22:25.574
The authenticated connector qualification
used Codex zero point one six

00:22:25.574 --> 00:22:30.510
two point zero, alpha two. It exercised
contextual advice and an isolated

00:22:30.510 --> 00:22:34.972
source edit through review, apply, exact
restore and cancellation.

00:22:35.103 --> 00:22:39.471
The PerfChecker MCP controller uses HTTP
two. The registered core

00:22:39.471 --> 00:22:43.569
and native Julia editor workload have
their own qualification

00:22:43.569 --> 00:22:47.802
records; consult the release report for
those separate results.

00:22:47.933 --> 00:22:51.536
A different supported server can supply
the same kind of tool workflow.

00:22:51.667 --> 00:22:55.125
Keep its tool schema, permissions and
provider configuration

00:22:55.125 --> 00:22:58.237
explicit, and use your package's
correctness tests and

00:22:58.237 --> 00:23:01.350
compatible measurements to assess the
proposed result.

00:23:02.277 --> 00:23:05.825
When you are ready, choose Prepare
implementation from reviewed advice.

00:23:05.956 --> 00:23:09.872
PerfChecker warns that generated changes
can be incorrect, saves

00:23:09.872 --> 00:23:13.727
a Git checkpoint of the on-disk state, and
prepares an isolated

00:23:13.727 --> 00:23:17.398
temporary checkout for the agent. Save
editor buffers first.

00:23:17.529 --> 00:23:21.759
The checkpoint captures files on disk, so
an unsaved buffer needs its

00:23:21.759 --> 00:23:25.927
own attention. Review the proposed
experiment and the implementation

00:23:25.927 --> 00:23:30.340
tool's access to the isolated copy. The
agent prepares its change there.

00:23:30.472 --> 00:23:34.635
You then inspect the diff and validation
before deciding whether to apply it

00:23:34.635 --> 00:23:38.798
to the original working tree. Keep the
measured baseline and the checkpoint.

00:23:38.929 --> 00:23:42.548
They serve different purposes: the
baseline supports your performance

00:23:42.548 --> 00:23:46.167
comparison, while the checkpoint supports
recovery of the code state.

00:23:46.298 --> 00:23:48.952
A useful proposal needs both a clear diff
and

00:23:48.952 --> 00:23:51.429
evidence that its semantics are preserved.

00:23:51.794 --> 00:23:55.278
Read the change in source before applying
it. We return to the

00:23:55.278 --> 00:23:58.987
same sum of squares operation that we
tested and measured earlier.

00:23:59.118 --> 00:24:02.133
The proposal avoids an intermediate array
by using

00:24:02.133 --> 00:24:04.907
a lazy reduction with an explicit
initializer.

00:24:05.038 --> 00:24:08.833
That initializer matters for empty input,
but the entire rewrite

00:24:08.833 --> 00:24:12.391
still needs validation for the package's
types and numerical

00:24:12.391 --> 00:24:16.068
semantics. Check what changed and what the
agent left assumed.

00:24:16.199 --> 00:24:19.473
Does the operation preserve the intended
result? Are the

00:24:19.473 --> 00:24:22.806
representative inputs covered? Does a
numerical reduction

00:24:22.806 --> 00:24:25.788
require a tolerance or preserve a
particular order?

00:24:25.919 --> 00:24:30.232
Are side effects or error cases part of
the public behavior? Inspect

00:24:30.232 --> 00:24:34.672
the proposed test and any actual oracle
output from the isolated copy.

00:24:34.672 --> 00:24:38.795
Passing one example cannot establish
equivalence for every input.

00:24:38.926 --> 00:24:42.485
The purpose of this review is to decide
whether the proposal and

00:24:42.485 --> 00:24:46.544
validation are adequate for your package.
Apply remains a human decision.

00:24:46.675 --> 00:24:50.059
A text recommendation, completed agent
turn or attractive

00:24:50.059 --> 00:24:53.028
diff does not automatically replace that
decision.

00:24:53.956 --> 00:24:58.508
Use the correctness protocol that you
reviewed. Preserve the package's existing

00:24:58.508 --> 00:25:02.656
tests and add representative cases where
the proposed change needs them.

00:25:02.787 --> 00:25:06.911
Check empty input and boundary behavior
when relevant, and use the

00:25:06.911 --> 00:25:10.971
actual types and tolerances required by
your API. For this sum of

00:25:10.971 --> 00:25:14.970
squares workload, the native oracle checks
three concrete cases.

00:25:15.101 --> 00:25:18.595
An empty Float sixty-four vector should
give zero point zero.

00:25:18.595 --> 00:25:21.861
The values one, minus two and three should
give fourteen.

00:25:21.992 --> 00:25:24.736
A Float sixty-four vector containing one
through one

00:25:24.736 --> 00:25:27.691
thousand should give three hundred
thirty-three million,

00:25:27.691 --> 00:25:30.330
eight hundred thirty-three thousand, five
hundred.

00:25:30.461 --> 00:25:34.576
The proposal already includes an
initializer; these assertions verify

00:25:34.576 --> 00:25:38.631
its behavior for the selected cases. When
the reviewed patch and its

00:25:38.631 --> 00:25:42.805
validation are adequate, choose Apply
reviewed implementation changes.

00:25:42.936 --> 00:25:46.365
Inspect the resulting source and run the
agreed correctness

00:25:46.365 --> 00:25:50.201
checks in the intended environment. Then
measure the same workload

00:25:50.201 --> 00:25:53.804
with the same relevant collector and
comparable configuration.

00:25:53.935 --> 00:25:56.980
Keep the before-and-after reports. A
correctness pass

00:25:56.980 --> 00:26:00.254
and a performance observation answer
different questions,

00:26:00.254 --> 00:26:03.528
and you need both to support a useful
optimization claim.

00:26:03.893 --> 00:26:08.126
After reviewing the diff, choose Apply
reviewed implementation changes.

00:26:08.126 --> 00:26:12.181
Now inspect the actual source and run the
agreed correctness oracle.

00:26:12.312 --> 00:26:15.852
Include the cases that motivated the
review, such as empty

00:26:15.852 --> 00:26:19.391
input or a representative element type.
Remeasure the same

00:26:19.391 --> 00:26:22.991
workload with comparable inputs, collector
and environment.

00:26:23.123 --> 00:26:26.911
Open the saved baseline and candidate
results, read their units,

00:26:26.911 --> 00:26:30.343
and check comparison compatibility before
claiming a gain.

00:26:30.474 --> 00:26:34.052
If you want to return to the checkpoint,
use Restore implementation

00:26:34.052 --> 00:26:37.522
checkpoint while the repository still
matches the expected state.

00:26:37.653 --> 00:26:41.102
A drift refusal protects later edits from
being silently

00:26:41.102 --> 00:26:44.735
overwritten; inspect it before recovering
content manually.

00:26:44.866 --> 00:26:48.611
The complete workflow is advice, review,
isolated proposal,

00:26:48.611 --> 00:26:52.483
diff, explicit apply, correctness and
performance validation,

00:26:52.483 --> 00:26:55.910
with a retained baseline and a recoverable
code state.

00:26:56.276 --> 00:27:00.493
Restore is scoped to the implementation
checkpoint and applied patch. It

00:27:00.493 --> 00:27:04.828
should not silently discard unrelated
edits made after the reviewed state.

00:27:04.959 --> 00:27:07.921
If the repository has changed, inspect the
refusal

00:27:07.921 --> 00:27:11.119
and the saved evidence before deciding how
to recover.

00:27:11.250 --> 00:27:15.321
Retain the source revision, selected
configuration, correctness result

00:27:15.321 --> 00:27:19.102
and performance reports for a change that
you keep. These records

00:27:19.102 --> 00:27:23.231
explain both what was measured and why you
accepted the implementation.

00:27:23.362 --> 00:27:26.660
Cancellation of a local agent worker and
interruption of

00:27:26.660 --> 00:27:29.841
an external server are also separate
responsibilities.

00:27:29.972 --> 00:27:33.979
Inspect the final status if an agent turn
or experiment is stopped,

00:27:33.979 --> 00:27:37.747
and keep diagnostics when cleanup is
incomplete. You can finish

00:27:37.747 --> 00:27:41.754
the workflow without an agent by applying
reviewed advice yourself.

00:27:41.885 --> 00:27:45.055
The same measurement and validation
protocol still matters.

00:27:45.186 --> 00:27:48.277
The optional connection adds a
conversation and an

00:27:48.277 --> 00:27:51.615
implementation proposal to a performance
investigation

00:27:51.615 --> 00:27:55.015
that already has explicit workloads and
saved evidence.

00:27:55.942 --> 00:28:00.457
We have taken a selected workload through
correctness, measurement, comparison,

00:28:00.457 --> 00:28:04.630
a reactive notebook, an agent conversation
and a reviewed implementation.

00:28:04.761 --> 00:28:07.980
The useful result is a decision that you
can explain and

00:28:07.980 --> 00:28:11.775
reproduce. Prepare the environments
deliberately, select the exact

00:28:11.775 --> 00:28:15.282
work, keep the measured baseline and
inspect units and scope.

00:28:15.413 --> 00:28:19.570
Use explicit launch and cancellation
controls, review source changes,

00:28:19.570 --> 00:28:23.546
and wait for cleanup before starting
again. Collector and analyzer

00:28:23.546 --> 00:28:27.763
availability depends on Julia, the
platform and prepared dependencies.

00:28:27.894 --> 00:28:31.379
The release report records native editor
versions and CPU

00:28:31.379 --> 00:28:34.987
architectures separately from the core
matrix. Check it for

00:28:34.987 --> 00:28:38.594
the environment you use, including the
stated macOS limits.

00:28:38.725 --> 00:28:43.623
The documentation provides installation,
the first check, suite comparisons,

00:28:43.623 --> 00:28:48.264
Pluto session controls and MCP
configuration. If something fails, report

00:28:48.264 --> 00:28:52.711
the versions, selected project, minimal
workload and redacted output.

00:28:52.842 --> 00:28:55.712
Start small, retain the evidence, and use
the next

00:28:55.712 --> 00:28:58.697
experiment to answer one clear performance
question.

